The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ret < 0) {
    dput(parent);
    dput(cur);
    return ERR_PTR(ret);
}
```
```c
if (len < 0) {
    dput(parent);
    dput(cur);
    return ERR_PTR(len);
}
```
```c
if (pos < 0) {
    return ERR_PTR(-ENAMETOOLONG);
}
```
Reason:
These code paths return early without calling `__putname(path)` to free the memory allocated by `__getname()` at the beginning of the function. Because ownership of the `path` buffer is not transferred to the caller (via `path_info->path`) in these error cases, the buffer is leaked, causing memory leaks.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
__putname(path);
return ERR_PTR(ret);
```
```c
__putname(path);
return ERR_PTR(len);
```
```c
__putname(path);
return ERR_PTR(-ENAMETOOLONG);
```
Reason:
Adding `__putname(path)` before each error return ensures that the dynamically allocated memory is freed. This prevents memory leaks by releasing the buffer when the function cannot complete successfully and will not pass the buffer to the caller.

